The  utility  is  an  authenticator for IEEE 802.11 networks.  It
provides full support for WPA/IEEE 802.11i and can also act as an
IEEE 802.1X Authenticator with a suitable backend  Authentication
Server  (typically The utility implements the authentication pro‐
tocols that piggyback on top of the normal IEEE  802.11  protocol
mechanisms.   To  use  as an authenticator, the underlying device
must support some basic functionality such as the ability to  set
security  information  in  the  802.11 management frames.  Beware
that not all devices have this support.  The utility is  designed
to be a program that runs in the background and acts as the back‐
end  component  controlling the wireless connection.  It supports
separate frontend programs such as the text‐based  frontend,  The
following  arguments  must  be specified on the command line: Use
the settings in the specified configuration file; the name of the
specified wireless interface is contained in this file.  See  for
a  description  of the configuration file syntax.  Changes to the
configuration file can be reloaded by sending a to the  processor
or with the utility, using The options are as follows: Enable de‐
bugging messages.  If this option is supplied twice, more verbose
messages  are  displayed.  Show help text.  Include timestamps in
debugging output.  Display version information  on  the  terminal
and exit.  Detach from the controlling terminal and run as a dae‐
mon process in the background.  Include key information in debug‐
ging output.  The utility first appeared in The utility was writ‐
